# @intra/web — Feature-Sliced Design

Next.js 16 App Router. Layers depend **strictly downward**:

```
app → widgets → features → entities → shared
```

An import that points upward (an `entity` importing a `feature`, a `feature` importing a `widget`)
is an architecture violation — lift the shared part down into `entities/` or `shared/` instead.

| Layer | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `app/` | App Router routes, root layout, providers, error/auth boundaries |
| `widgets/` | Page-level compositions (dashboard, sidebar, report pages, comment threads) |
| `features/` | One user flow each (create cycle, archive cycle, post comment) |
| `entities/` | Domain primitives per bounded context (cards, tables, badges, types) |
| `shared/` | HTTP client, hooks, utils, shadcn primitives, app-wide UI |

## Slice anatomy

Every `entities/<context>/<entity>/` and `features/<context>/<entity>/<flow>/` slice repeats the
same triple. Keep it — do not flatten a slice into a single file:

```
api/      axios call sites + react-query hooks
model/    TS types, zod schemas, mappers, contexts
ui/       presentational components for this slice only
```

Context folders mirror the API bounded contexts exactly: `feedback360`, `identity`, `library`,
`organisation`, `reporting`.

## Conventions

- Server Components by default; add `'use client'` only where interactivity or hooks demand it.
- Data fetching goes through the shared axios client and react-query hooks in the slice's `api/`.
  No `fetch` calls scattered in components.
- Forms use react-hook-form + zod; the schema belongs in the slice's `model/`.
- UI primitives come from `shared/ui` (shadcn, new-york style, Lucide icons). Do not add a second
  component library or hand-roll a primitive that already exists there.
- Types shared with the backend come from `@intra/shared-kernel` (transpiled by `next.config.ts`),
  never redeclared locally.

## Path aliases

`@/*` and `@src/*` → `src/*`, plus `@entities/*`, `@features/*`, `@widgets/*`, `@shared/*`,
`@lib/*`, `@public/*`. Use them instead of deep relative paths.
